# Azure DevOps: CI Pipeline for a Microservices Application

## 1. Project Overview — Example Voting App

The application used in this project is a **multi-microservice voting app** (originally from Docker samples). It lets users vote between two options and view real-time results.

**Repository:** Clone or import `dockersamples/example-voting-app` from GitHub.

### Run Locally

```bash
docker compose up
```

- Vote app: `http://localhost:5000`
- Results app: `http://localhost:5001`

---

## 2. What is Continuous Integration?

**Continuous Integration (CI)** is the practice of automatically validating every code change as soon as it is pushed to a version control system.

### Typical CI pipeline stages

```
Developer pushes code
        │
        ▼
[CI Pipeline triggers automatically]
        │
        ├── Unit Testing         → catch logic errors early
        ├── Static Code Analysis → enforce code quality standards
        ├── Build                → compile / package the application
        ├── Docker Image Build   → containerise the application
        └── Push to Registry     → store the image in ACR / Docker Hub
```

### Why CI matters

- Removes the need for manual validation after every commit.
- Gives reviewers confidence before merging a pull request.
- Catches bugs early — before they reach staging or production.
- CI runs on both **pull requests** (pre-merge validation) and **commits** (post-merge full pipeline).

---

## 3. Understanding the Application Architecture

| Microservice | Language | Role |
|---|---|---|
| **vote** | Python | Frontend — accepts user votes, writes to Redis |
| **redis** | — | In-memory data store — queues votes between vote and worker |
| **worker** | .NET | Reads votes from Redis, persists them to PostgreSQL |
| **db** | PostgreSQL | Persistent database storing vote counts |
| **result** | Node.js | Frontend — reads from PostgreSQL, displays results |

**CI is needed only for custom code:**

- ✅ `vote` (Python) — needs CI
- ✅ `worker` (.NET) — needs CI
- ✅ `result` (Node.js) — needs CI
- ❌ `redis` — uses a public Docker image, no custom code
- ❌ `db` — uses a public Docker image, no custom code

**One independent CI pipeline per microservice** — each can be deployed independently without affecting others. This is a core microservices principle.

---

## 4. Writing Dockerfiles for Each Microservice

### Result Service (Node.js)

```dockerfile
FROM node:18-alpine

WORKDIR /usr/local/app

RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends tini curl

COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm install

COPY . .

ENTRYPOINT ["/sbin/tini", "--"]
CMD ["node", "server.js"]
```

### Vote Service (Python)

```dockerfile
FROM python:3.11-slim

WORKDIR /app

COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install -r requirements.txt

COPY . .

CMD ["python", "app.py"]
```

### Worker Service (.NET)

```dockerfile
ARG TARGETPLATFORM
ARG TARGETARCH
ARG BUILDPLATFORM

FROM --platform=$BUILDPLATFORM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:7.0 AS build

WORKDIR /source
COPY *.csproj .
RUN dotnet restore -a $TARGETARCH

COPY . .
RUN dotnet publish -c Release -o /app -a $TARGETARCH --no-restore

FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/runtime:7.0
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=build /app .
ENTRYPOINT ["dotnet", "Worker.dll"]
```

> **Key principle:** Choose the base image to match the application's runtime. Use the dependency file (`requirements.txt`, `package.json`, `.csproj`) to install dependencies inside the image. The entry point command starts the application — confirm this with the development team.

---

## 5. Setting Up Azure DevOps Project and Repos

### Create a new project

1. Go to [dev.azure.com](https://dev.azure.com) → Create project.
2. Name: `example-voting-app` → **Private** → Create.

### Import the GitHub repository into Azure Repos

1. Go to **Repos** → **Import repository**.
2. Source type: **Git**
3. Clone URL: `https://github.com/dockersamples/example-voting-app`
4. No authentication required (public repo).
5. Click **Import**.

### Fix the default branch

Azure Repos may set an incorrect default branch alphabetically. Fix it:

1. Go to **Repos** → **Branches**.
2. Find `main` → click the three-dot menu → **Set as default branch**.

---

## 6. Creating an Azure Container Registry (ACR)

The CI pipeline pushes Docker images to ACR after building them.

1. Azure Portal → search **Container Registry** → **Create**.
2. Fill in:
- **Resource Group:** `azure-cicd` (create new)
- **Registry name:** e.g., `myaciregistry` *(must be globally unique)*
- **SKU:** Standard
3. Click **Review + Create** → **Create**.

> The ACR registry name becomes part of the image URL: `<registry-name>.azurecr.io/<image-name>:<tag>`

---

## 7. Azure Pipeline YAML Structure

Every Azure Pipeline YAML file follows this structure:

```yaml
trigger:           # When should this pipeline run?
  paths:
    include:
- result     # Only trigger when files in /result change

variables:         # Reusable values (registry connection, image names)
  dockerRegistryServiceConnection: '<service-connection-id>'
  imageRepository: 'result-app'
  containerRegistry: '<registry>.azurecr.io'
  dockerfilePath: 'result/Dockerfile'
  tag: '$(Build.BuildId)'

stages:            # High-level groupings of work
- stage: Build
    jobs:          # Units of work within a stage
- job: BuildImage
        pool:
          name: azure-agent   # Agent pool to run this job on
        steps:     # Individual commands / tasks within a job
- task: Docker@2
            inputs:
              command: build
              ...
```

### Key sections

| Section | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `trigger` | Defines when the pipeline runs (on push, PR, schedule, path change) |
| `variables` | Reusable values — registry name, image name, file paths |
| `stages` | High-level phases — Build, Test, Push, Deploy |
| `jobs` | Work units within a stage; can run in parallel on different agents |
| `steps` | Individual tasks/commands within a job |
| `pool` | Specifies which agent (Microsoft-hosted or self-hosted) runs the job |

---

## 8. Setting Up a Self-Hosted Agent (Linux VM)

Microsoft-hosted agents are not available on free trial accounts. A self-hosted agent (a VM running on Azure) is used instead.

### Step 1 — Create a VM

- Azure Portal → **Virtual Machines** → **Create**
- Resource Group: `azure-cicd`
- Name: `azure-agent`
- Image: Ubuntu 22.04 LTS
- Authentication: SSH key (download `.pem`)

### Step 2 — Create an Agent Pool in Azure DevOps

1. Azure DevOps → **Organisation Settings** (bottom left) → **Agent pools**.
2. Click **Add pool** → **Self-hosted** → Name: `azure-agent`.
3. ✅ Grant access permission to all pipelines → **Create**.

### Step 3 — Create a Personal Access Token (PAT)

1. Azure DevOps → top-right user icon → **Personal access tokens**.
2. **New Token** → Name: `azure-agent-token` → Expiry: 30 days → Scope: **Full access**.
3. **Copy the token immediately** — it won't be shown again.

### Step 4 — Configure the agent on the VM

SSH into the VM:

```bash
chmod 600 ~/Downloads/azure-agent.pem
ssh -i ~/Downloads/azure-agent.pem azureuser@<VM-PUBLIC-IP>
```

Run the following commands inside the VM:

```bash
# Update repositories
sudo apt update

# Download the agent binary
mkdir myagent && cd myagent
wget https://vstsagentpackage.azureedge.net/agent/<version>/vsts-agent-linux-x64-<version>.tar.gz
tar zxvf vsts-agent-linux-x64-<version>.tar.gz

# Configure the agent
./config.sh
```

When prompted:

| Prompt | Value |
|---|---|
| Server URL | `https://dev.azure.com/<your-organisation>` |
| Authentication type | PAT |
| Personal Access Token | *(paste the token copied above)* |
| Agent pool | `azure-agent` |
| Agent name | `azure-agent` |
| Work folder | *(press Enter for default)* |

### Step 5 — Install Docker on the agent VM

```bash
sudo apt install docker.io -y

# Grant azureuser permission to run Docker without sudo
sudo usermod -aG docker azureuser

# Restart Docker
sudo systemctl restart docker

# Verify Docker works as azureuser
docker pull hello-world
```

### Step 6 — Start the agent

```bash
cd myagent
./run.sh
```

**Verify:** Azure DevOps → Agent pools → `azure-agent` → **Agents** tab → status should show **Online**.

> Keep `./run.sh` running in the terminal during pipeline execution. For production, configure the agent as a system service using `./svc.sh install && ./svc.sh start`.

---

## 9. Writing the CI Pipeline — Results Service

**File:** `azure-pipelines-result.yml`

```yaml
trigger:
  paths:
    include:
- result

variables:
  dockerRegistryServiceConnection: '<your-service-connection-id>'
  imageRepository: 'result-app'
  containerRegistry: '<your-registry>.azurecr.io'
  dockerfilePath: 'result/Dockerfile'
  tag: '$(Build.BuildId)'

stages:
- stage: Build
    displayName: Build Image
    jobs:
- job: Build
        displayName: Build
        pool:
          name: azure-agent
        steps:
- task: Docker@2
            displayName: Build the Docker image
            inputs:
              command: build
              repository: $(imageRepository)
              dockerfile: $(dockerfilePath)
              containerRegistry: $(dockerRegistryServiceConnection)
              tags: |
                $(tag)

- stage: Push
    displayName: Push Image
    jobs:
- job: Push
        displayName: Push
        pool:
          name: azure-agent
        steps:
- task: Docker@2
            displayName: Push the Docker image
            inputs:
              command: push
              repository: $(imageRepository)
              containerRegistry: $(dockerRegistryServiceConnection)
              tags: |
                $(tag)
```

---

## 10. Writing the CI Pipeline — Vote Service

**File:** `azure-pipelines-vote.yml`

The structure is identical to the result pipeline. Change only:

```yaml
trigger:
  paths:
    include:
- vote          # ← changed

variables:
  imageRepository: 'vote-app'       # ← changed
  dockerfilePath: 'vote/Dockerfile' # ← changed
  # all other variables remain the same
```

---

## 11. Writing the CI Pipeline — Worker Service

**File:** `azure-pipelines-worker.yml`

```yaml
trigger:
  paths:
    include:
- worker         # ← changed

variables:
  imageRepository: 'worker-app'        # ← changed
  dockerfilePath: 'worker/Dockerfile'  # ← changed
  # all other variables remain the same

stages:
- stage: Build
    displayName: Build Image
    jobs:
- job: Build
        pool:
          name: azure-agent
        steps:
- task: Docker@2
            displayName: Build Worker Image
            inputs:
              command: build
              repository: $(imageRepository)
              dockerfile: $(dockerfilePath)
              containerRegistry: $(dockerRegistryServiceConnection)
              tags: |
                $(tag)
              # Note: If the Dockerfile uses ARG TARGETPLATFORM / TARGETARCH,
              # pass build args or simplify the Dockerfile for single-arch builds.
```

> **Worker Dockerfile note:** The worker Dockerfile uses `ARG TARGETPLATFORM`, `ARG TARGETARCH`, and `ARG BUILDPLATFORM` for multi-architecture builds. If building for a single architecture (Linux), either pass the build args via `--build-arg` or simplify the Dockerfile to target `linux/amd64` directly to avoid build failures.

---

## 12. Validating Path-Based Triggers

Path-based triggers ensure that only the relevant pipeline runs when code in a specific folder changes.

### Test: Change in `worker/` folder — only `worker` pipeline should trigger

1. Go to **Repos** → `worker/Dockerfile`.
2. Make a minor edit (e.g., add a blank line) → **Commit**.
3. Go to **Pipelines** — only `worker-service` pipeline should trigger.
4. `result-service` and `vote-service` pipelines should **not** trigger.

### Test: Change in `result/` folder — only `result` pipeline should trigger

1. Go to **Repos** → `result/server.js`.
2. Make a minor edit → **Commit**.
3. Go to **Pipelines** — only `result-service` pipeline should trigger.

---

## 13. Troubleshooting Notes

| Issue | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `permission denied while connecting to Docker daemon` | `azureuser` not in `docker` group yet | Run `sudo usermod -aG docker azureuser`, restart Docker, log out and log back in |
| `cannot find a pool with name azure-agent` | Agent pool not created or agent offline | Create the pool in Org Settings, start `./run.sh` on the VM |
| `cannot configure agent: already configured` | Running `./config.sh` twice | Skip config, just run `./run.sh` directly |
| Worker build fails: `invalid platform specifier` | Missing `TARGETPLATFORM` build arg | Set `TARGETPLATFORM=linux` in the Docker task or simplify the Dockerfile |
| Agent offline after Docker install | Permissions applied but session not refreshed | Log out and SSH back into the VM, then restart `./run.sh` |

---

## 14. Summary

| Concept | Key Takeaway |
|---|---|
| CI pipeline | Automated validation — build, test, containerise, and push on every code change |
| Path-based trigger | Only triggers the relevant microservice pipeline when files in that folder change |
| Self-hosted agent | A VM registered with Azure DevOps to run pipeline jobs (required on free tier) |
| Azure Container Registry | Private Docker registry on Azure where built images are pushed |
| One pipeline per microservice | Each service is independently built and deployed — core microservices principle |
| Pipeline YAML sections | `trigger` → `variables` → `stages` → `jobs` → `steps` |

### What was achieved

- ✅ Migrated source code from GitHub to Azure Repos.
- ✅ Created Azure Container Registry for storing Docker images.
- ✅ Set up a self-hosted Linux agent on an Azure VM.
- ✅ Wrote CI pipelines for all three microservices (result, vote, worker).
- ✅ Implemented path-based triggers so only the changed microservice pipeline runs.
- ✅ Successfully built and pushed Docker images to ACR.

